        <p class="kicker">Journal</p>
        <h1>Why the listings are incomplete</h1>
        <p class="lede">If you search only in English, you are seeing a display window. The shop is larger, and some of what is in the shop should not be sold.</p>
      </div>
    </header>
    <article class="section">
      <div class="wrap measure">
        <p>Peru has listing sites. Urbania and Adondevivir are the ones city buyers open first. They are useful. They are not the market. A great deal of property moves because someone knows someone, or because a phone number is painted on a wall.</p>
        <h2>Se vende</h2>
        <p>The sign is often literal: <span lang="es">se vende</span>, a number, sometimes a price that has been crossed out. The number may be the owner, a relative, or a person who collects buyers. The sign may be a year old. It is still worth a look and a call. It is not worth a deposit. I treat the sign as an introduction to a file, and the file has to survive the registry.</p>
        <h2>The cousin</h2>
        <p>Word of mouth is how houses move in towns where nobody bothers with a portal. The advantage is that you hear about a place before it is dressed up for a foreigner. The risk is that “my uncle is selling” skips the spouse, the heirs, or the co-owner. Charm is not a <span lang="es">partida registral</span>.</p>
        <h2>Brokers, portals, and groups</h2>
        <p>A local broker who walks that district can save weeks. A broker who only forwards WhatsApp albums cannot. The portals show asking prices, which is a start for benchmarking and a bad end if you think the asking price is a sale. Facebook groups are faster and worse: reposts, stale photos, and a price that crept up because the audience is foreign. That creep is what I mean by gringo pricing. I compare it with ranges I know from buying and selling. I do not publish the ranges as a table, because a table goes stale and starts to look like a promise.</p>
        <h2>What the photo cannot hold</h2>
        <p>Noise, the neighbor’s workshop, the dump beyond the wall, the river, the pesticide schedule, the road in the rain, the tank that is empty by evening. Those are why <a href="../scout.html">scouting</a> is the service, and why a live call beats a gallery. Video tours of places, as a journal, are something I intend to add. They are not on this page today.</p>
        <h2>Off-market, said plainly</h2>
        <p>I look for properties that are not dressed for an English ad, when they match what you said you need. Off-market is not a magic discount. It is a way to see the local price before it is performed. Some of those properties are unsellable for a reason. The work is telling the difference, with a lawyer on the paper and a person on the ground.</p>
        <p>If you want to see how thin the public shelf is in a specific town, say which town on the <a href="../contact.html">intake</a>. If I do not know it well enough to walk, I will say that too.</p>
      </div>
    </article>
